1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Three ingredients: how many pieces a problem splits into, how much smaller each piece is, and what the splitting costs.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Written T(n) = a T(n/b) + f(n): a pieces, each of size n/b, plus f(n) for the divide and the recombine.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:24,000
The theorem answers one question: which part of the work dominates, the top, the levels, or the leaves.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:32,000
It is not a formula to memorise. It is a comparison between two quantities, and the comparison is the whole method.

5
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:40,000
At depth i the tree has a^i subproblems, each of size n / b^i. That is the whole structure.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Subproblems stop when the size reaches 1. Halving 1024 ten times takes 10 levels: k = log base b of n.

7
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,000
So the leaves number a^k = a^(log_b n) = n^(log_b a). At a = 4, b = 2 that is n squared: 1048576 leaves.

8
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Call it the watershed. Every case of the theorem is a comparison between f(n) and this number.

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Take a = 4, b = 2, f(n) = n. Each level down multiplies the work by a/b, which is 2.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
At n = 1024 the levels cost 1024, 2048, 4096, and so on up to 1048576 at the leaves.

11
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The last level alone is n squared, larger than everything above it. The sum is 2n squared minus n: 2096128.

12
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:36,000
So T is Theta of n squared, which is Theta of n^(log_b a). The leaves pay the bill: case 1.

13
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Same a and b, but f(n) = n squared. Level i costs 4^i times n squared over 4^i: the 4s cancel.

14
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Eleven levels, each costing 1048576. No level dominates, so the count is one level times the number of levels.

15
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The sum is n squared times (k + 1) = 11534336. T is Theta of n squared log n: the log is the height of the tree.

16
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Case 2: f equals the watershed. The answer is the watershed times a log, and the constant in front is 1.

17
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Same a and b once more, f(n) = n cubed. Level i costs 4^i times n cubed over 8^i: the work halves each level.

18
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:26,000
The root costs 1073741824. Everything below it adds up to less than the root again.

19
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,000
The exact sum is 2146435072, just under 2n cubed. T is Theta of n cubed, which is Theta of f(n).

20
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Case 3: the root pays the bill. This is the one case that carries a second condition, and scene 7 is that condition.

21
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:52,000
One picture: the watershed n^(log_b a) against f(n), both drawn on log-log axes so a power is a straight line.

22
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Below: the leaves win. Equal: the levels tie and a log appears. Above: the root wins.

23
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Below and above mean by a polynomial factor: f is at most the watershed over n^epsilon, for one fixed epsilon above zero.

24
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
The page classifies every (a, b, p) on a 72-entry grid and checks each answer numerically. All 72 agree.

25
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Case 3 has a second hypothesis: a times f(n/b) is at most c times f(n), for some c below 1 and every large n.

26
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:28,000
In words: each level down costs less than the one above, by a fixed fraction. That is what makes the series converge.

27
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:36,000
For f(n) = n^p the ratio is exactly a / b^p. So c below 1 is the same statement as p above log_b a.

28
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:46,000
So for a polynomial f the condition is not extra work: it is case 3 own polynomial gap. At a = 8, b = 2, p = 3 the ratio is exactly 1, the boundary.

29
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Now the case the theorem cannot answer: f(n) = n / log n, with a = 2, b = 2. The watershed is n.

30
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Case 1 would need n / log n at most n over n^epsilon, that is n^epsilon over log n bounded. It is not bounded.

31
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:10,000
So no epsilon works, and case 1 does not apply. Neither does case 2 or 3: f is not equal to n, and it is not polynomially larger.

32
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Unrolling gives level weights n/k, n/(k-1), and so on up to n/1: a harmonic sum. T = n(1 + H_k), which is Theta of n log log n.

33
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:30,000
One more boundary, on the other side: f(n) = n log n with a = 2, b = 2. f is neither equal to n nor polynomially larger.

34
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Extended case 2: if f is the watershed times log^k n, the answer is the watershed times log^(k+1) n. One extra log.

35
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Here T = n(1 + k(k+1)/2) exactly. Divided by n k squared it is 0.578 at k = 8, falling to 0.5: Theta of n log squared n.

36
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Checked for k = 0, 1 and 2: f equal to n, n log n, n log squared n give n log n, n log squared n, n log cubed n.

37
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The theorem applies to a T(n/b) + f(n) and to nothing else. Three shapes it refuses, and each needs its own method.

38
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Subtract and conquer: T(n) = 2T(n-1) + n 2^n. The size falls by one, not by a factor, so b does not exist.

39
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:16,000
A domain transform: T(n) = root n times T(root n) + n. The coefficient is not a constant, so a does not exist.

40
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:24,000
And a non-polynomial gap: f between the watershed over n^epsilon and the watershed. Unroll, or substitute a guess and prove it.

41
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:32,000
GATE CSE 2014, Set 2: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + log n. The watershed is n, and a log is polynomially below any power: case 1.

42
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:40,000
GATE CSE 2024, Set 1: T(n) = root n T(root n) + n. Not the theorem shape: substituting n = 2^(2^m) gives n log log n.

43
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:48,000
GATE CSE 2025, Set 1: T(n) = 2T(n-1) + n 2^n. Subtract and conquer: unrolling gives a half n squared 2^n.

44
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Four steps: identify a, b and f; compute the watershed; place f; then check the case own condition before writing the answer.

